course: technology
lesson: 33
title: Wind, sun and other clean energy
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Some energy never runs out. The wind keeps blowing, the Sun keeps shining, the Moon keeps pulling the tides and the deep Earth stays hot; energy from sources like these is called renewable, and using it makes far less carbon dioxide than burning coal or gas.
explain: A wind turbine is a giant windmill. The wind pushes its long blades round, the blades turn a shaft, and a generator in the box at the top makes electricity, just like in a power station but with no fuel at all.
explain: A solar panel does something even stranger: nothing moves. Light hits thin slices of silicon and knocks tiny electrons loose, and they flow away as electricity, a bit like the little panel on a solar calculator that keeps working as long as there is light.
explain: The hard part is that wind and sun come and go. On a calm night there is neither, so the grid still needs other sources and stores of energy, and that is why engineers mix many kinds together.
fact: A wind turbine's blades are turned by the wind and spin a shaft and a generator in the box at the top; the biggest offshore turbines have blades more than 100 metres long each.
fact: Offshore wind farms are rows of turbines standing in the sea, where the wind is stronger and steadier, and cables carry the electricity to land; the UK has some of the largest in the world.
fact: In a solar panel, light hits special silicon cells and knocks tiny electrons free, which flow as electricity; panels still work on cloudy days and in winter, just less.
fact: Never climb onto a roof to touch solar panels; installers are trained to work up there safely.
fact: A solar farm is a field of panels, sometimes with sheep grazing between them, and some panels turn slowly to follow the Sun across the sky.
fact: A solar garden light charges a small battery from a little panel during the day, and a light sensor switches it on when it gets dark.
fact: A tidal power station uses the tides flowing in and out to spin underwater turbines, and tides are caused mainly by the Moon pulling on the sea.
fact: Geothermal energy uses heat from deep underground to warm water, make steam or heat homes; Iceland, a land of volcanoes with hot rock and hot water close under the ground, gets most of its heating this way.
fact: In a biogas plant, bacteria break down food waste and manure and make a gas that can be burned for electricity or heat, so a food waste bin can end up making electricity.
fact: Solar water heating panels are black because black things soak up sunlight and get hotter than white things, and the water in their pipes is warmed.
fact: Renewable energy comes from sources that do not run out, like wind, sun, water and underground heat; on some windy days wind gives more than half of the UK's electricity.
fact: We cannot use only wind and sun yet, because they come and go with the weather and the time of day, so the grid also needs storage and other sources.
exercise: What turns the generator in a wind turbine? | The blades, pushed round by the wind | A motor in the tower that runs on petrol or diesel | Batteries hidden inside the tall tower, charged at night | The heat of the Sun warming the metal of the tower
exercise: How long can the blades of the biggest offshore turbines be? | More than 100 metres each | About 10 metres each | About as long as a car | About 1 kilometre each
exercise: Why are many wind farms built out at sea? | The wind there is stronger and steadier | The sea cools the turbines down | Fish turn the blades as they swim | There is no space left on land anywhere in the world
exercise: In a solar panel, what does light do to the silicon cells? | Knocks tiny electrons free, which flow as electricity | Heats water inside the panel into steam that spins a turbine | Spins a tiny wheel hidden inside each cell, like a dynamo | Turns the silicon into a battery that fills up slowly all day
exercise: Do solar panels work on a cloudy day? | Yes, but they make less electricity | No, they stop completely | Yes, they make more than on sunny days | Only if the clouds are white
exercise: Why do some solar farm panels move slowly during the day? | They turn to follow the Sun | The wind pushes them round a little at a time | Sheep keep bumping into the legs as they graze | They tip over to shake off the rain and the dust
exercise: How does a solar garden light know when to come on? | A light sensor notices it getting dark | A clock inside is set to switch on at 8 o'clock | It feels the ground get colder | It hears the birds stop singing
exercise: What causes the tides that turn tidal turbines? | Mainly the Moon pulling on the sea | The wind blowing the water | Ships pushing the waves | Rivers emptying into the sea
exercise: What is geothermal energy? | Heat from deep underground | Energy from falling leaves | Energy from ocean waves | Light from the stars
exercise: Which country gets most of its heating from underground heat? | Iceland | Egypt | Australia | The Netherlands
exercise: What makes the gas in a biogas plant? | Bacteria breaking down food waste and manure | Solar panels on the roof heating the waste until it boils | Workers burning old tyres and plastic in a big furnace | Water pumped into a tank and squeezed until it fizzes
exercise: Why are solar water heating panels black? | Black things soak up more sunlight and get hotter | Black paint is the cheapest colour | Black keeps birds off the panels | Black reflects the most light
exercise: What does renewable energy mean? | Energy from sources that do not run out | Energy that is cheaper than all the other kinds | Energy that is used only once | Energy from new coal mines
exercise: Why can't we use only wind and sun yet? | They come and go with weather and time of day | They make too much carbon dioxide | They work only in summer | They are against the law in most countries of the world
exercise: On some windy days, how much of the UK's electricity can come from wind? | More than half | Almost none | Exactly one tenth | All of it, every day
test: «solar panels are useless in England, it's always cloudy innit» What is the best correction? | They still work on cloudy days, just less | You are right, they only work in full bright sunshine | They work only in summer at noon | They work only if the clouds are white
test: In a cartoon, a giant blows on a wind turbine to light up a city at night. What is true about real wind turbines? | The wind turns the blades and a generator makes electricity | The turbines blow the wind to make clouds | The turbines need people to push them | They light the city with lamps on the blades
test: What would happen to a solar garden light if you covered its panel with a box for a week? | Its battery would not charge, so it would stop lighting up | It would glow brighter than before | It would charge from the warmth of the ground instead of light | Nothing, it runs on air
test: «tides are just the wind pushing the sea about» What is the best correction? | Tides are caused mainly by the Moon pulling the sea | Right, the wind pushes the sea in and out every single day | Tides come from whales and big fish all swimming together | Tides come from rain filling the sea up and then draining away
test: Count them: a small offshore wind farm has 4 rows with 5 turbines in each row. How many turbines? | 20 | 9 | 25 | 15
test: Count them: each turbine has 3 blades. How many blades on 6 turbines? | 18 | 9 | 12 | 24
test: Count them: a solar farm has 3 strips of grass between its rows of panels, with 4 sheep grazing on each strip. How many sheep? | 12 | 7 | 16 | 9
test: «we put our potato peelings in the food bin, so what, it just rots» What can really happen? | Bacteria can turn it into biogas that makes electricity | It is burned straight away in a power station with the coal | It is fed into the wind turbines to make them spin faster | It is packed into rockets and sent up to the Moon to rot there
test: Two bottles of water lie in the sun, one painted white and one painted black. Which warms faster, and why? | The black one, because black soaks up more sunlight | The white one, because white soaks up more sunlight | Both the same, colour does not matter | The white one, because it reflects heat inwards
test: «why not just switch off every gas station tomorrow and use wind» What is the main problem? | On calm nights there is little wind or sun, so we still need other sources and storage | Wind turbines are too quiet to work at night | Wind makes more pollution than gas | The wind would run out after a week
test: Why does Iceland find it easy to heat homes with geothermal energy? | It has a lot of hot rock and hot water close under the ground | It is the sunniest country in Europe | It has the most wind turbines of any country in the whole world | It burns ice to make heat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises renewable energy? | It derives from sources not exhausted by use, such as wind and sunlight | It is derived exclusively from coal and oil of very recent extraction from the ground | It is energy which may be employed on one occasion only and is then lost for ever | It is energy which is cheaper than every alternative in every country of the world
exam: Identify the principal process by which a solar cell produces electricity. | Light liberates electrons within the silicon, which then flow as an electric current | Light heats water into steam which rotates a turbine | Light rotates a small wheel concealed within the panel | Light is stored in the form of a chemical fuel
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of the tides employed in tidal power stations. | The gravitational attraction exerted by the Moon upon the oceans | The action of the wind blowing steadily upon the surface of the sea | The discharge of rivers into the sea | The movement of large marine animals
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the impossibility, at present, of relying solely upon wind and solar energy. | Their output varies with the weather and the time of day, so storage and other sources are required | They emit more carbon dioxide than coal | Their use is prohibited in the majority of countries | They operate exclusively during the summer months
